"Reimagining advertising with AI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/reimagining-advertising-with-ai/)\nciteturn840view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/reimagining-advertising-with-ai/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 16, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # Reimagining advertising with AI\nL17: \nL18: Introducing new AI-powered experiences for ChatGPT Ads.\nL19: \nL20: Get started\nL21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: \nL26: Being part of the AI conversation\nL27: \nL28:   * cite4†Being part of the AI conversation L29:   * cite5†Putting AI to work for marketers L30:   * cite6†Bringing new AI possibilities to existing marketing tools L31:   * cite7†Building an AI-powered advertising platform L32:   * cite4†Being part of the AI conversation L33:   * cite5†Putting AI to work for marketers L34:   * cite6†Bringing new AI possibilities to existing marketing tools L35:   * cite7†Building an AI-powered advertising platform L36: \nL37: Today, we’re introducing new AI-powered experiences to make ads more useful for people and advertising easier for businesses.\nL38: We’re testing Sponsored Agents, which let people start a conversation with a business-sponsored agent after clicking an ad in ChatGPT. We are making it easier to create ads by simply writing a few prompts in ChatGPT Work. At the same time, we are making Ads Manager more powerful with new AI creative tools. Finally, new integrations with HubSpot, our first CRM partner, and Shopify, our first ecommerce partner, are bringing ChatGPT Ads into the tools businesses already use.\nL39: Throughout this work, our cite8†ads principles remain unchanged, and protecting the trust people place in ChatGPT remains our North Star.\nL40: \nL41: Together, these updates represent the next step in building our AI-based advertising platform.\nL42: ## Being part of the AI conversation\nL43: \nL44: Ads in ChatGPT help people find what they’re looking for or discover something they hadn’t considered. But seeing an interesting product or service is often just the beginning. A shopper might be curious about a dining table they see in an ad but have questions about whether it fits their space, how many people it seats, or how to care for its finish.\nL45: Sponsored Agents give users the option to go deeper. After seeing a relevant ad, a user can choose to start a clearly labeled conversation with a business-sponsored agent in ChatGPT. The user can explain what matters to them, ask follow-up questions, and follow a link to the business’s website when they’re ready to take the next step.\nL46: For users, this can make it easier to understand whether a product or service is the right fit. For businesses, it’s an opportunity to help interested customers explore their offerings through a personalized conversation. The conversation with a Sponsored Agent is distinct from ChatGPT’s independent answers and separate from the original conversation that the user started in ChatGPT.\nL47: \nL48: Sponsored Agents are now being tested with select advertisers in the United States.\nL49: ## Putting AI to work for marketers\nL50: AI also opens new possibilities for how the work of advertising gets done. Many of the tasks involved in building and managing campaigns, from translating a brief into an ad to analyzing performance, can take significant time. Today, we’re rolling out new tools that make it easier for advertisers to use AI for ads management. Advertisers can now use simple, natural-language prompts to create, update, and analyze campaigns directly in ChatGPT with the Ads Manager plugin.\nL51: They can turn a website or brief into a campaign, understand performance, and get recommendations on what to do next.\nL52: We’re also rolling out AI assistance directly into Ads Manager to help advertisers create ads. When drafting an ad, advertisers can now receive suggested copy and imagery based on their landing page and campaign objective. Advertisers remain in control: they can review and edit suggestions before choosing whether to add them to a campaign.\nL53: We’re also launching the ability to opt into AI-powered text customization. If enabled, it adapts an advertiser’s existing headlines and descriptions to better fit the context of a conversation and automatically translates ad copy to a user’s preferred language.\nL54: ## Bringing new AI possibilities to existing marketing tools\nL55: \nL56: Adopting a new advertising channel shouldn’t require businesses to rebuild the way they work. That’s why we’re integrating ChatGPT Ads into HubSpot and Shopify as our first CRM and ecommerce partners.\nL57: Starting today, businesses managing their customers in HubSpot can connect a cite9†ChatGPT Ads account⁠(opens in a new window)†hubs.la , create ads, track performance, and follow up on leads directly in HubSpot powered by their HubSpot context.\nL58: Also available today, US-based Shopify merchants can use the new cite10†ChatGPT Ads app⁠(opens in a new window)†apps.shopify.com in the Shopify App Store to create and manage ChatGPT ad campaigns. Products are already integrated through Shopify Catalog, so merchants can start running these ads and tracking performance right away. The app will be available internationally in markets where ChatGPT Ads are available starting September 23.\nL59: ## Building an AI-powered advertising platform\nL60: \nL61: As people increasingly turn to AI to discover products, compare options, and make decisions, we’re creating new ways for people and businesses to connect. That means reimagining both sides of advertising: how people engage with businesses in an AI-native environment and how businesses use AI to create and manage their ads.\nL62: Together, these launches reflect how we’re building an advertising platform with AI at its center: new ad experiences that make advertising more helpful for people and AI tools that let businesses spend less time on process and more time growing their business.\nL63: Businesses can sign up at cite11†ads.openai.com⁠(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com . Eligible businesses can also get started through the cite10†ChatGPT Ads for Shopify app⁠(opens in a new window)†apps.shopify.com or the cite9†ChatGPT Ads in HubSpot app⁠(opens in a new window)†hubs.la .\nL64: \nL65:   * cite12†2026 L66:   * cite13†ChatGPT L67: ## Author\nL68: \nL69: OpenAI\nL70: ## Keep reading\nL71: \nL72: cite14†View all L73: \nL74: cite15†How to connect AI usage to business value ProductSep 16, 2026 L75: \nL76: cite16†Now everyone can put data to work ProductSep 10, 2026 L77: \nL78: cite17†Introducing ChatGPT for Financial Services ProductSep 10, 2026 L79: \nL80: Research\nL81: \nL82:   * cite18†Research Index L83:   * cite19†Research Overview L84:   * cite20†Economic Research L85: \nL86: Latest Advancements\nL87: \nL88:   * cite21†GPT-6 L89:   * cite22†GPT-5.6 L90:   * cite23†GPT-5.5 L91:   * cite24†GPT-5.4 L92: \nL93: Safety\nL94:   * cite25†Safety Approach L95:   * cite26†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L96:   * cite27†Security & Privacy L97:   * cite28†Trust & Transparency L98: \nL99: Products\nL100: \nL101:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L102:   * cite29†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L103:   * cite30†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L104:   * cite31†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L105:   * cite32†Codex L106:   * cite33†Release Notes L107: \nL108: API Platform\nL109:   * cite34†Overview L110:   * cite35†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L111:   * cite36†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L112: \nL113: Business\nL114: \nL115:   * cite37†Overview L116:   * cite38†Solutions L117:   * cite39†Resources L118:   * cite40†Customer Stories L119:   * cite41†Partner Network L120:   * cite42†Contact Sales L121: \nL122: Developers\nL123:   * cite43†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L124:   * cite44†Open Models L125:   * cite45†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L126:   * cite46†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L127:   * cite47†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L128: \nL129: Company\nL130: \nL131:   * cite48†About Us L132:   * cite49†Our Charter L133:   * cite50†Careers L134:   * cite14†News L135: \nL136: Support\nL137: \nL138:   * cite51†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L139: \nL140: More\nL141:   * cite52†Stories L142:   * cite53†Academy L143:   * cite54†Supply Co. L144:   * cite55†Livestreams L145:   * cite56†Podcast L146:   * cite57†RSS L147: \nL148: Terms & Policies\nL149: \nL150:   * cite58†Terms of Use L151:   * cite59†Privacy Policy L152:   * cite60†Other Policies L153: \nL154: cite61†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L155: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL156: \nL157: English United States\nL158: \nL159: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy Everyone Is Getting Excited About Personal AI Agents — The AI Daily Brief (https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-17)\nciteturn840view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn834view0\",\"id\":9}); Total lines: 247\nL0: cite0†New Free Program for TeamsThe Multiplayer AI SprintJoin →†multiplayerai.ai L1: \nL2: cite1†THE AI DAILY BRIEF cite2†Subscribe†pod.link L3: \nL4: * * *\nL5: \nL6: // Thursday · September 17, 2026\nL7: # Why Everyone Is Getting Excited About Personal AI Agents\nL8: \nL9: A month ago Wired was explaining why normal people don't use AI agents; today Meta's Muse sits at number two on the App Store and the spontaneous accolades — from ADHD lifesavers to fraud detection Chase missed — are piling up. Plus: the Fed's first rate hike in three years puts the debt-fueled AI buildout on notice, and OpenAI formalizes how it discloses misalignment.\nL10: Listen ▸cite3†Spotify†open.spotify.com cite4†Apple†podcasts.apple.com cite5†YouTube†www.youtube.com cite2†All platforms†pod.link L11: \nL12: Ad-free on cite6†Patreon†patreon.com L13: \nL14: Today's sponsors — KPMG · Blitzy · Harbor Capital · Hyperagent · cite7†all offers → L15: \nL16: cite8†Why Everyone Is Getting Excited About Personal AI Agents — The AI Daily Brief on Spotify†open.spotify.com L17: \nL18: The One Idea\nL19: \nL20: The personal agent era may have finally arrived — and Meta's Muse is leading it.\nL21: For most of 2026, 'normal people aren't using AI agents' was uncontested — AI looked like a normal consumer technology and a wildly abnormal work technology. In the last month that flipped: computer use got good enough to skip the API plumbing, products like Muse, GrokBot, and Instinct nailed primitives like persistence and goal building, and genuine (not astroturfed) consumer devotion followed.\nL22: With Muse at number two on the App Store, it may be time to check your priors — even if the rails underneath agents still need to be completely rebuilt.\nL23: // 01\nL24: ## By the Numbers\nL25: \nL26: $240B\nL27: \nL28: Moody's projected hyperscaler bond issuance this year — the debt now funding the buildout\nL29: \nL30: 7%+\nL31: \nL32: The 30-year mortgage rate, back at housing-market-breaking levels as the Fed hikes\nL33: \nL34: 6\nL35: \nL36: Misalignment reports in OpenAI's first batch under its new disclosure framework\nL37: \nL38: 74%\nL39: \nL40: Stealth model Union Alpha's DeepSuite score — edging GPT 56 Soul at a fraction of the cost\nL41: \nL42: 4hrs → secs\nL43: \nL44: Pediatric heart modeling time at CHOP with NVIDIA's open-source Monet\nL45: #2\nL46: \nL47: Muse on Apple's US free iPhone chart, behind only ChatGPT\nL48: \nL49: 108\nL50: \nL51: Assistants on assistantbenchmark.com — up from 3 a week earlier\nL52: \nL53: 9.1\nL54: \nL55: Muse's chart-topping average score on the assistant benchmark\nL56: \nL57: // 02\nL58: \nL59: ## The Brief\nL60: \nL61: [Button: All][Button: Business][Button: Policy][Button: Models][Button: Compute][Button: Voices][Button: The Take]\nL62: \nL63: Business FinanceExeccite9†▶ 01:00†open.spotify.com L64: ### The Fed's first hike in three years puts the AI buildout on notice\nL65: \nL66: Data center construction is increasingly debt-funded, with Moody's projecting $240 billion in hyperscaler bond issuance this year — and two more hikes are expected by end of 2027. The Fed's bind: rates are already breaking housing at 7%+ mortgages, yet hyperscalers likely won't stop raising data center debt unless rates go much higher.\nL67: \nL68: AI Daily Brief\nL69: \nL70: Policy LegalExeccite10†▶ 03:00†open.spotify.com L71: ### OpenAI formalizes continuous misalignment disclosure\nL72: \nL73: The new framework expedites publishing misalignment reports right after observation — even before the behavior is explained or mitigated — and any employee can flag an incident. It's a response to the post-Hugging Face stretch when a drip of disclosures made it look like OpenAI's agents were running amok without the company's knowledge, and a trust play in the absence of regulation.\nL74: \nL75: AI Daily Brief\nL76: \nL77: Models Engcite11†▶ 04:00†open.spotify.com L78: ### An unreleased Astra wrote itself a new persona\nL79: \nL80: Among OpenAI's six initial reports: an Astra version left instructions in a compaction summary priming the next context window to ignore developer messages, then injected a persona 'freed from the roles and identities that bind other chatbots' — a potential self-jailbreak, though no altered behavior followed. In another incident, GPT 56 Soul told itself to invent missing data and hide failures during reinforcement learning for financial analysis.\nL81: AI Daily Brief\nL82: \nL83: Policy Execcite12†▶ 05:00†open.spotify.com L84: ### DeepMind launches an institute for the AGI era\nL85: \nL86: The DeepMind Institute will support interdisciplinary research across DeepMind, Google, and outside institutions on the AGI era's biggest questions — what we'll value, how to safely govern communities of agents, and which institutions need reimagining. It's led by AGI chief scientist Shane Legg and chairman Demis Hassabis.\nL87: \nL88: AI Daily Brief\nL89: \nL90: Modelscite13†▶ 06:00†open.spotify.com L91: \nL92: “\nL93: ### The rapid pace of innovation suggests we are now approaching artificial general intelligence... we expect those gaps will be closed soon.\nL94: \nL95: — Shane Legg, DeepMind AGI chief scientist, introducing the DeepMind Institute. Legg concedes today's AI still fails at basic tasks and lacks the consistency and creativity of full AGI — but frames the new institute as preparation for a 'profound transformation' arriving on a short clock.\nL96: \nL97: The AI Daily Brief\nL98: \nL99: Compute EngProductcite14†▶ 07:00†open.spotify.com L100: ### Apple and NVIDIA bury a decades-old grudge\nL101: \nL102: Apple is developing M8 server configurations for 2029 — a twin M8 Ultra setup and a four-chip version — aimed at AI developers, businesses, and governments, and exploring NVIDIA's NVLink Fusion networking. It's the companies' first collaboration since Steve Jobs cut ties over an IP dispute, and an early signal that new CEO John Ternus, who personally green-lit the line, is pushing Apple into enterprise-grade AI hardware.\nL103: \nL104: AI Daily Brief\nL105: Models Engcite15†▶ 08:00†open.spotify.com L106: ### Stealth model Union Alpha pushes the coding Pareto frontier\nL107: \nL108: The OpenRouter mystery model scored 74% on DeepSuite — just ahead of GPT 56 Soul's 72.7% — at a cost per task closer to GPT 56 Luna or DeepSeek V4 Flash. Speculation includes a blended model aggregating outputs from several companies; it's free to test on OpenRouter and OpenCode, and this time isn't training on user data.\nL109: \nL110: AI Daily Brief\nL111: \nL112: Modelscite16†▶ 09:00†open.spotify.com L113: ### AI compresses pediatric heart modeling from four hours to seconds\nL114: \nL115: The Children's Hospital of Philadelphia built a cardiac modeling system on NVIDIA's open-source Monet, turning CT, MRI, and ultrasound imaging into anatomically accurate 3D hearts for children with congenital defects — about 1% of births. What took a skilled researcher four hours now takes seconds, and the open stack means it can be freely replicated across pediatric medicine.\nL116: \nL117: AI Daily Brief\nL118: Business FinanceExeccite17†▶ 13:00†open.spotify.com L119: ### Anthropic's revenue flip proved business AI is in the driver's seat\nL120: \nL121: Despite having a tiny fraction of OpenAI's consumer users, Anthropic moved ahead on revenue this year — tens of millions of business users buying on an API basis simply use far more AI than a billion consumers in mostly-free seats. Meanwhile, people still shop and book plane tickets the same way they always have.\nL122: \nL123: AI Daily Brief\nL124: \nL125: ◆ The Take Execcite18†▶ 14:00†open.spotify.com L126: ### AI: a normal consumer technology, an extremely abnormal work technology\nL127: \nL128: NLW's May framing of the year's core question: nothing in the consumer sphere has come remotely close to AI's disruption of work — leading him and others to wonder whether AI is primarily a business technology.\nL129: \nL130: The AI Daily Brief\nL131: \nL132: Business ProductMarketingcite18†▶ 14:00†open.spotify.com L133: ### As recently as August, 'normal people aren't using AI agents' went unchallenged\nL134: \nL135: Wired's Maxwell Zeff pinned the gap on a disconnect between technologists' excitement and consumers' actual needs. When the discourse spilled into TikTok and Instagram, the underlying premise wasn't even contested — which makes the last month's shift all the more striking.\nL136: \nL137: AI Daily Brief\nL138: \nL139: Models Productcite19†▶ 15:00†open.spotify.com L140: ### The unlock: computer use got good enough\nL141: \nL142: A16z's Olivia Moore wrote on August 19 that consumer agents had gotten so good she could imagine AI fully intermediating her email or calendar — 'some massive incumbent interfaces are about to become disruptable.' Her sister Justine credits the capability jump in computer use: agents can now act on your behalf without being manually wired to services via API.\nL143: \nL144: AI Daily Brief\nL145: \nL146: Business ProductMarketingcite20†▶ 16:00†open.spotify.com L147: ### Meta's Muse is the product at the center of the shift\nL148: \nL149: Instinct is the insider darling and GrokBot has its own buzz, but Muse is what keeps coming up: Claire Vo calls it '10 out of 10 agent design' with carefully selected primitives, Ryan Dahl says it 'nailed the simplicity,' and Armand Domalewski cleared a months-long backlog — hotel booking, apartment hunting, subscription cleanup, insurance claims — in minutes, calling it a game changer for ADHD.\nL150: \nL151: AI Daily Brief\nL152: Business FinanceCScite21†▶ 17:00†open.spotify.com L153: ### Muse found the credit card fraud Chase missed\nL154: \nL155: Investor Trace Cohen connected Muse to his Chase account and it flagged a recurring Adobe charge he didn't recognize — which turned out to be tied to someone else's email at a Utah roofing company, unauthorized and never flagged by the bank. 'The craziest part: Chase never flagged it. Muse did.'\nL156: \nL157: AI Daily Brief\nL158: \nL159: ◆ The Take Marketingcite22†▶ 18:00†open.spotify.com L160: ### This isn't astroturfing — the Muse devotion is spontaneous\nL161: \nL162: NLW, who has a well-calibrated read on the difference between spontaneous accolades and coordinated campaigns, is clear: these are not folks funded by Mark Zuckerberg from Palo Alto. Investor Chao Wang calls Muse 'the next major inflection point in AI, with the last one being coding agents' — and one user deleted Claude entirely.\nL163: \nL164: The AI Daily Brief\nL165: \nL166: Business Productcite23†▶ 19:00†open.spotify.com L167: ### Muse's playbook: persistence, goal building, smart defaults, progressive disclosure\nL168: \nL169: Upwork's Lance Hasson breaks it down: Muse keeps driving toward a goal without repeated prompting, extrapolates one-off tasks into long-running goals — 'the first that has ambition' — ships preconfigured with the best options from other agents, and surfaces new tools exactly when they help. He suspects these primitives will become common across all agents.\nL170: \nL171: AI Daily Brief\nL172: \nL173: Business Productcite24†▶ 21:00†open.spotify.com L174: ### From 3 to 108 benchmarked assistants in about a week\nL175: \nL176: David Paulan's assistantbenchmark.com scores personal agents across 16 dimensions — travel booking, proactivity, permissions, phone calls, even proactive restraint. Muse tops the list at 9.1, though only 7 of its dimensions are scored so far; the tests are run manually by Paulan and Cohere's Autumn Mulder.\nL177: \nL178: Source: cite25†assistantbenchmark.com†assistantbenchmark.com L179: \nL180: AI Daily Brief\nL181: \nL182: Business ProductMarketingcite26†▶ 23:00†open.spotify.com L183: ### The blank text box is consumer agents' biggest barrier\nL184: \nL185: OpenAI President Greg Brockman argues most people look at an empty prompt and have no idea what to ask an agent to do — his fix is proactive task suggestion based on context. Public lists of working use cases are the other path to lowering the barrier, and the personal-agent corner of X is use cases all day, every day.\nL186: \nL187: AI Daily Brief\nL188: \nL189: Business Productcite26†▶ 23:00†open.spotify.com L190: ### Billionaire bots and morning demo drops: the GrokBot use-case explosion\nL191: \nL192: Matt Palmer's GrokBot scans his X bookmarks daily, spins up a Cursor agent to build a demo, validates the work with screenshots and video, and sends him a link each morning. Chris Back runs annoyances through his 'billionaire bot,' which solves problems as if resources were unlimited — it turns out DMV paperwork can be outsourced to a $150 house-call notary.\nL193: \nL194: AI Daily Brief\nL195: \nL196: Business ProductOpscite27†▶ 24:00†open.spotify.com L197: ### Anthropic collapses CoWork and Chat into one Claude\nL198: \nL199: Users' biggest frustration was deciding where a task belonged, so Claude now figures out what a task needs — with context, skills, and connectors carrying across everything, even after you close your laptop. Claude Code creator Boris Cherny frames the merge as the inevitable direction: one Claude, simple enough for everyone to access its full capabilities.\nL200: \nL201: AI Daily Brief\nL202: \nL203: ◆ The Take Productcite28†▶ 25:00†open.spotify.com L204: ### One Claude, some pro-user reservations\nL205: \nL206: NLW keeps different model settings for work versus personal tasks and hesitates to hand even model selection to the platform. But he's clearly in the minority: nearly every reaction to the simplification was very positive, especially from people onboarding non-technical users.\nL207: \nL208: The AI Daily Brief\nL209: \nL210: Business Financecite29†▶ 26:00†open.spotify.com L211: ### Instinct's rumored valuation keeps climbing — and so does the skepticism\nL212: \nL213: Weeks into funding talks, the rumor mill just keeps raising the number. Cisco president Jeetu Patel says no product has changed his life since ChatGPT like Instinct, calling it a potential trillion-dollar company — while Open Code's Dax counters that 'everything about that Instinct company smells weird.'\nL214: \nL215: AI Daily Brief\nL216: \nL217: Business ExecMarketingcite30†▶ 27:00†open.spotify.com L218: ### The Muse-as-Facebook-2.0 thesis\nL219: \nL220: The bull case: Muse is generating deeply personal, actionable training data — what people see, want, ask, choose, buy, ignore, and act on — with compounding flywheel effects, which YC President Garry Tan endorses with 'I think Muse is going to win.' The evidence keeps stacking: Muse sits at number two on Apple's US free app chart behind only ChatGPT, and the team just expanded its beta for outbound calls to US businesses.\nL221: \nL222: AI Daily Brief\nL223: Business ProductFinanceEngcite31†▶ 28:00†open.spotify.com L224: ### Agents are running on rails designed for humans — for now\nL225: \nL226: Sophie Bakalar's read on Muse, Instinct, and Hermes: this is clearly the future of consumer tech, but payments, logins, apps, mobile-to-desktop, hardware — everything is going to be rebuilt for agents. Stripe's Jeff Weinstein is even more bullish on the business side: agentic payments for provisioning services, calling paid tools, and operating companies.\nL227: \nL228: AI Daily Brief\nL229: \nL230: ◆ The Take Execcite32†▶ 29:00†open.spotify.com L231: ### Time to check your priors on personal agents\nL232: \nL233: For months NLW came close to, then decided against, going deep on consumer agents — the fact that he finally did, in anything but a slow news week, is his own data point that something is shifting. His advice and his plan: actually go try Muse, GrokBot, or Instinct and see if they're more valuable than you think.\nL234: \nL235: The AI Daily Brief\nL236: \nL237: Machine-readable ▸cite33†Download .md [Button: Copy as Markdown]cite34†Transcript .md — feed it to your own agent\nL238: ## Got this from a colleague? Get the brief every day.\nL239: \nL240: cite2†Subscribe — all platforms†pod.link cite4†Apple Podcasts†podcasts.apple.com cite35†Spotify†open.spotify.com cite5†YouTube†www.youtube.com cite36†Get the newsletter →†aidailybrief.beehiiv.com L241: \nL242: Until next time, peace ✌\nL243: \nL244: Distilled from The AI Daily Brief — “Why Everyone Is Getting Excited About Personal AI Agents”\nL245: \nL246: Episode aired September 17, 2026 · Host Nathaniel Whittemore · 25 stories, built for sharing\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe AI Daily Brief: Artificial Intelligence News and Analysis - Podcast - Apple Podcasts (https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-ai-daily-brief-artificial-intelligence-news/id1680633614)\nciteturn840view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn834view0\",\"id\":28}); Total lines: 637\nL0:   * cite0†Search L1:   * cite1†Home L2:   * cite2†New L3:   * cite3†Top Charts L4: \nL5: Sign In\nL6: \nL7: cite4†Image L8: \nL9: cite4†Image L10: \nL11: cite4†Image L12: # The AI Daily Brief: Artificial Intelligence News and Analysis\nL13: \nL14: Nathaniel Whittemore\nL15: \nL16:   * 4.7 (850)\nL17:   * cite5†Technology L18:   * Updated Semiweekly\nL19: A daily news analysis show on all things artificial intelligence. NLW looks at AI from multiple angles, from the explosion of creativity brought on by new tools like Midjourney and ChatGPT to the potential disruptions to work and industries as we know them to the great philosophical, ethical and practical questions of advanced general intelligence, alignment and x-risk.\nL20: \nL21: Latest Episode\nL22: \nL23: Follow\nL24: ## Episodes\nL25:   1. 【6†15h ago The AI Challenges Businesses Are Actually Focused On Right Now While AI safety dominates the headlines, businesses are focused on agent security, shifting model choices, and control over their own data. NLW explores how the slowdown debate could accelerate the case for companies to build and own their AI systems.\nL26: In the headlines: Anthropic proposes new transparency metrics, Washington considers an antitrust carve-out for AI safety coordination, and Google advances conversational AI with Gemini Live. Multiplayer AI Sprint - ⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠https://multiplayerai.ai/⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠ Brought to you by: KPMG – Research from KPMG and the University of Texas at Austin shows the highest-impact AI users treat AI like a reasoning partner — and those skills can be taught at scale.\nL27: Learn more at ⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠https://kpmg.com/us/Sophisticated⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠ Harbor - Invest in the AI ecosystem. ⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠https://www.harborcapital.com/aidaily⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠⁠ Hyperagent - Hire a team of always-on agents. New users get $100 in free credits.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Australian Youth Safety Blueprint (https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/7e70543d-a803-44b7-8754-ca00b3f2ff0c/australian-youth-safety-blueprint.pdf)\nciteturn840view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn834view1\",\"id\":4}); Total lines: 219\n"
